<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RUT_TwilightSea.xml                    UtinniPatches · authored 2026-09-06
  ============================================================================
  LIQUID_BIOMES_MAP_1 — OFFLINE HALF. One of the item's two "brine seas",
  per design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/terminator_sea.md §2: "the Twilight
  Sea (θ 91, bearing 170, moldy) and the Grey Sea (θ 92, bearing 8,
  salt-encrusted, shrinking). Both are hypersaline; both are terminal sinks
  with no outflow." Reconciled against world/ASHKARR_WORLDMAP_tiles.csv:
  region "Twilight Sea" is 852 tiles total, biome breakdown Ocean 442 /
  SeaIce 171 / five land biomes on the shore (Desert 140, AridShrubland 50,
  ZBiome_Badlands 16, Wasteland 14, ExtremeDesert 14, AB_RockyCrags 5).
  This def concerns the 442 Ocean-biome (liquid) tiles only — SeaIce is
  frozen margin, not a liquid biome, and out of this item's scope; the
  shore's land biomes are a separate, already-existing assignment this item
  does not touch.

  🔴 NOT ParentName="Ocean". Same trap as RUT_TheScald.xml / RUT_NightsideIce.xml:
  Core/Defs/BiomeDefs/Biomes_Water.xml's <BiomeDef> tag for Ocean carries no
  Name="..." attribute, so ParentName="Ocean" would silently fail to
  resolve. Shape restated directly instead (isWaterBiome/canBuildBase/
  allowRoads/allowRivers/hasVirtualPlants/texture/impassable/
  isBackgroundBiome/workerClass copied verbatim from vanilla Ocean, same
  file, lines 4-17).

  Why a new def at all, since Ocean's 823 tiles split EXACTLY 442/381
  between Twilight Sea and Grey Sea with nothing left over (verified against
  the same CSV) — patching vanilla Ocean in place would not leak onto any
  third region the way patching Lake would (see RUT_TheScald.xml's "Damp"
  finding): terminator_sea.md §4 rules "each sea is an island, so each sea
  is endemic and alone... every large organism in a terminator sea exists in
  that sea and nowhere else in the universe" — a single shared Ocean patch
  cannot carry two different, mutually exclusive faunas. Twilight and Grey
  need their own defNames even though they could technically share terrain.

  Water terrain: deliberately UNCHANGED from vanilla (oceanDeepTerrain/
  oceanShallowTerrain left unset, inheriting WaterOceanDeep/WaterOceanShallow
  -> waterBodyType Saltwater). "Hypersaline" is real chemistry in the design
  doc but the engine has exactly one water-salinity tier beyond Freshwater
  (Verse.WaterBodyType: None/Freshwater/Saltwater/Other — enumerated via
  ilprobe this pass), so Saltwater already says everything the engine can
  say; no new TerrainDef is warranted the way the Scald's actual burn
  mechanic (RUT_ScaldWater.xml) was. A visually distinct "dull steel and
  bone, never blue" water texture (terminator_sea.md §9's artistic theme) is
  real but separate, unowed art work, not a mechanical need — noted here so
  it is not lost, not invented here.

  wildAnimals / coastalWildAnimals / fishTypes: left EMPTY, documented, same
  reasoning as RUT_TheScald.xml. terminator_sea.md's roster consequences
  (§ "Roster consequences") ask for "a single mat/film organism per sea" and
  "one or two enormous, ancient, solitary brine animals per sea, endemic to
  it" but names no defNames — that creature-authoring pass (with its own art
  and normalization-law review) is SEAS_WATERLINE_PASS_1 /
  SW_SEA_MONSTERS_ART_1 territory, not this item's.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <BiomeDef>
    <defName>RUT_TwilightSea</defName>
    <label>the Twilight Sea</label>
    <description>A hypersaline terminal sea on the terminator band, moldy from shore to shore with one mat organism that has never had to share it. It has no outflow and it is shrinking — the stranded shoreline terraces mark where it used to reach.</description>
    <workerClass>BiomeWorker_Ocean</workerClass>
    <isWaterBiome>true</isWaterBiome>
    <canBuildBase>false</canBuildBase>
    <!-- allowRoads TRUE by owner ruling 2026-09-07: "All of our biomes should allow roads." 91 authored road links crossed RUT_NightsideIce and were invisible - the links were structurally valid (asymmetric 0, nonAdjacent 0), the biome simply refused to draw them. -->
    <allowRoads>true</allowRoads>
    <allowRivers>false</allowRivers>
    <hasVirtualPlants>false</hasVirtualPlants>
    <texture>World/Biomes/Ocean</texture>
    <impassable>true</impassable>
    <isBackgroundBiome>true</isBackgroundBiome>
    <!-- WILD ANIMALS, landed 2026-09-09 by BIOME_FAUNA_ASSIGNMENT_SITTING_1 from
         design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/rosters/the_twilight_sea.json. This
         supersedes the "left EMPTY, SEAS_WATERLINE_PASS_1's job" note above: the
         sea beasts now exist as defs and the roster names them.
         The Lanternwhale is THE gardener - one giant, endemic, single-spawn
         (terminator_sea §4: every large organism endemic to its own sea); the
         Aerofleet is a shore/shadow-lane drifter shared with the Grey Sea, which is
         legal because endemism binds GIANTS and fliers range (freeze R17).
         🔴 No <li>: BiomeAnimalRecord's custom loader reads the node NAME as the
         animal and the node TEXT as the commonality; an <li> discards the whole def.
         ⚠️ This def declared NO animalDensity, and the engine default is 0 - so the two
         residents above could never spawn, on a dive map or anywhere else. CLOSED
         2026-09-09 (fix wave, BIOME_FAUNA_ASSIGNMENT_SITTING_1): 0.1 declared below. -->
    <wildAnimals>
      <AA_Aerofleet MayRequire="sarg.alphaanimals">0.05</AA_Aerofleet>                    <!-- aerofleet - drifter, import -->
      <RSW_Lanternwhale MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.swbestiary">0.005</RSW_Lanternwhale>     <!-- lanternwhale - endemic giant, import -->
    </wildAnimals>
    <!-- animalDensity: undeclared (engine default 0) -> 0.1, DESIGNED interim
         (2026-09-09 fix wave). terminator_sea.md's population doctrine is endemism and
         scarcity - every large organism endemic to its own sea, one Lanternwhale
         gardener, a shared shore drifter - so the sea is sparsely peopled by rule, not
         empty. 0.1 matches vanilla SeaIce (MEASURED from the def dump; IceSheet 0.2,
         Desert 0.4), the closest shipped water/ice analog. ⚠️ A quicktest tune is OWED,
         and the diving-mods pass may revise it once dive maps actually spawn. -->
    <animalDensity>0.1</animalDensity>
    <coastalWildAnimals />

    <!-- GRAVTIDE SUPPORT, added 2026-09-07 (UNDERWATER_BIOME_SUPPORT_1).
         GravTide's arrival gate is generic - OceanArrivalPatches.IsOceanTile and
         OceanTileFeatures.IsWater both test PrimaryBiome.isWaterBiome - so this sea
         already qualifies for a gravship landing and a dive. What it lacked is the
         three things GravTide patches onto vanilla Ocean/Lake BY defName, which never
         reach a modded biome:
           1. terrainsByFertility - THE CRITICAL ONE. MapGenUtility.TerrainFrom() finds
              no terrain without it, logs "No terrain found in biome <X>" and falls back
              to TerrainDefOf.Sand, which is why an unpatched water biome generates an
              all-SAND map instead of a sea.
           2. baseWeatherCommonalities - otherwise every landing logs
              "All weather commonalities were zero. Defaulting to Clear."
           3. wildAnimalsCanWanderInto false - land animals have nowhere to walk from.
         Depth comes from NEGATIVE elevation (OceanTileFeatures.DepthOf = max(0,-elev));
         these tiles sit at -350 m, so ~350 m: past the ~150 m high-pressure nervous
         syndrome threshold, inside drysuit range (800 m). -->
    <wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>false</wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>
    <baseWeatherCommonalities>
      <Clear>12</Clear>
      <Fog>7</Fog>
      <Rain>4</Rain>
    </baseWeatherCommonalities>
    <terrainsByFertility>
      <li>
        <terrain>WaterOceanDeep</terrain>
        <min>-999</min>
        <max>999</max>
      </li>
    </terrainsByFertility>
  </BiomeDef>

</Defs>
